                "text": "Analyse the examples:\n- Input is 10x10.\n- Output is 20x20.\n- Non-zero pixels become 4x4 blocks in the output.\n- Notice the rows and columns in the output:\n  - Rows with non-zero pixels in the input:\n    - In Train 1:\n      - r=3 (c=9, val=3) -> output block at rows 4..7, cols 16..19\n      - r=5 (c=3, val=8) -> output block at rows 8..11, cols 4..7\n      - r=7 (c=5, val=1) -> output block at rows 12..15, cols 8..11\n      - r=9 (c=1, val=8; c=7, val=2) -> output block at rows 16..19, cols 0..3 (for c=1) and cols 12..15 (for c=7)\n    - Train 2:\n      - r=1 (c=7, val=9) -> rows 0..3, cols 12..15\n      - r=3 (c=3, val=5; c=5, val=9) -> rows 4..7, cols 4..7 (c=3) and cols 8..11 (c=5)\n      - r=9 (c=5, val=5; c=9, val=7) -> rows 16..19, cols 8..11 (c=5) and cols 16..19 (c=9)\n    - Train 3:\n      - r=1 (c=3, val=1) -> rows 0..3, cols 4..7\n      - r=3 (c=1, val=6; c=5, val=4) -> rows 4..7, cols 0..3 (c=1), cols 8..11 (c=5)\n      - r=5 (c=7, val=5) -> rows 8..11, cols 12..15\n      - r=9 (c=5, val=4; c=7, val=8) -> rows 16..19, cols 8..11 (c=5), cols 12..15 (c=7)\n\nWait, what are the row coordinates?\nIn Train 1:\nInput rows with pixels: 3, 5, 7, 9.\nOutput block rows:\nr=3 -> block row index 1 (rows 4..7, since 1*4=4)\nr=5 -> block row index 2 (rows 8..11, 2*4=8)\nr=7 -> block row index 3 (rows 12..15, 3*4=12)\nr=9 -> block row index 4 (rows 16..19, 4*4=16)\n\nNotice input rows: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9?\nWait:\nr=1 -> block row 0: rows 0..3: (1-1)/2 = 0!\nr=3 -> block row 1: rows 4..7: (3-1)/2 = 1!\nr=5 -> block row 2: rows 8..11: (5-1)/2 = 2!\nr=7 -> block row 3: rows 12..15: (7-1)/2 = 3!\nr=9 -> block row 4: rows 16..19: (9-1)/2 = 4!\n\nColumns:\nInput cols with pixels:\nTrain 1:\nc=1 -> cols 0..3 -> block col 0 -> (1-1)/2 = 0\nc=3 -> cols 4..7 -> block col 1 -> (3-1)/2 = 1\nc=5 -> cols 8..11 -> block col 2 -> (5-1)/2 = 2\nc=7 -> cols 12..15 -> block col 3 -> (7-1)/2 = 3\nc=9 -> cols 16..19 -> block col 4 -> (9-1)/2 = 4\n\nAll non-zero pixels are at odd row and odd column!\nr in {1, 3, 5, 7, 9}, c in {1, 3, 5, 7, 9}!\nThe grid of odd coordinates is a 5x5 grid: (r-1)/2, (c-1)/2.\nThen each cell in this 5x5 grid is scaled up by 4x4 to make a 20x20 grid!\nLet's verify:\n5x5 scaled by 4x4 gives 20x20!\nLet's double check if only odd coordinates have non-zero pixels:\nTrain 1:\n(3, 9)=3 -> (1, 4)=3 -> rows 4..7, cols 16..19. Correct.\n(5, 3)=8 -> (2, 1)=8 -> rows 8..11, cols 4..7. Correct.\n(7, 5)=1 -> (3, 2)=1 -> rows 12..15, cols 8..11. Correct.\n(9, 1)=8 -> (4, 0)=8 -> rows 16..19, cols 0..3. Correct.\n(9, 7)=2 -> (4, 3)=2 -> rows 16..19, cols 12..15. Correct.\n\nTrain 2:\n(1, 7)=9 -> (0, 3)\n(3, 3)=5 -> (1, 1)\n(3, 5)=9 -> (1, 2)\n(9, 5)=5 -> (4, 2)\n(9, 9)=7 -> (4, 4)\nMatches output perfectly!\n\nTrain 3:\n(1, 3)=1 -> (0, 1)\n(3, 1)=6 -> (1, 0)\n(3, 5)=4 -> (1, 2)\n(5, 7)=5 -> (2, 3)\n(9, 5)=4 -> (4, 2)\n(9, 7)=8 -> (4, 3)\nMatches output perfectly!\n\nRule is:\nSample the input at odd rows and columns: input[2*i + 1][2*j + 1] for i in 0..4, j in 0..4.\nThen repeat each element 4x4 to form a 20x20 output.I am ready to receive the test input.",
